    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, recurrent, severe (F33.2)</content>. PHQ-9 19, from 21; too early to judge the effect of sertraline. Tolerating it. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm; safety plan in place. [S]<list><item>Continue sertraline 50 mg daily.</item><item>Weekly psychotherapy.</item><item>PHQ-9 at four weeks; consider a dose increase then if the response is partial.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Tourette syndrome (F95.2)</content>. The presenting tic disorder now has a diagnosis. Dr. Hassan confirms Tourette syndrome: multiple motor tics and at least one vocal tic since childhood, present for decades, with premonitory urges and brief suppressibility, which distinguish tics from chorea. Yale Global Tic Severity Scale: motor 17, phonic 13, total tic score 30 of 50, impairment 20 of 50. Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale 13, mild, with counting and threshold rituals of the tic-related, just-right kind. No ADHD on screening. He was relieved to hear that tics are not madness. [S]<list><item>Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics, eight sessions.</item><item>No tic medication now.</item><item>Tic severity scale at three and six months.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression measure (PHQ-9)</td><td>At baseline and at least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>19, from 21</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Sertraline adherence and adverse effects</td><td>Every visit in the acute phase; reassess at 2 weeks after starting and after each dose change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two-week check: every dose taken; nausea for four days, resolved; no activation</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Interaction review (serotonergic drugs; NSAIDs and anticoagulants with an SSRI)</td><td>At the start and with any new medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No other medicines; told to avoid tramadol, triptans without advice, St. John&apos;s wort, and regular NSAIDs</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review (PHQ-9 item 9 and structured questions since the last visit), safety plan, and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or self-harm; nurse phone check done at one week</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Psychotherapy (cognitive behavioral therapy with behavioral activation)</td><td>Offer to all with moderate or severe depression</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two sessions</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood Disorder Questionnaire</td><td>Once at the start of a depression work-up</td><td>Up to date</td><td>3 at intake, negative</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Anxiety measure (GAD-7)</td><td>At baseline and with PHQ-9 reviews</td><td>Up to date</td><td>12 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sodium on an SSRI</td><td>Baseline; recheck if symptoms or age 65 or older</td><td>Up to date</td><td>140 mmol/L at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Tic severity (Yale Global Tic Severity Scale, recorded in the note; no LOINC code is used)</td><td>At diagnosis and periodically to judge the need for and response to treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Total tic score 30 (motor 17, phonic 13); impairment 20</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Assessment for co-occurring conditions (obsessive-compulsive symptoms, ADHD, anxiety, depression)</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Obsessive-compulsive symptoms mild (Y-BOCS 13); ADHD screen negative; depression and anxiety under treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Psychoeducation about tics for him and, if he wishes, his household</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Natural history of tics explained; tics are not a sign of losing his reason</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics</td><td>Offer as an initial treatment option</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Eight sessions with a trained therapist</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Tic-suppressing medication (alpha-2 agonist, aripiprazole, or other)</td><td>When tics cause impairment that behavioral therapy does not relieve</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Behavioral therapy first; he prefers to avoid more medicine</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Neurology consultation</td><td>At the start of evaluation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Hassan today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>PHQ-9 response (at least a 50% fall) by three months and remission (below 5) by six months. In progress; target 2026-09-28 [S]</item><item>Back at daily writing and seeing friends again by three months. In progress; target 2026-06-22 [S]</item><item>Fewer and less disruptive tics, judged by him and by the tic severity scale, by six months. In progress; target 2026-09-28 [S]</item><item>Safety plan in place and reviewed at every visit. In progress; target 2026-09-28 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Practice guideline recommendations summary: Treatment of tics in people with Tourette syndrome and chronic tic disorders (Pringsheim T, et al. Neurology 2019;92(19):896-906) (2019); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Visit in four weeks. Psychotherapy weekly. Behavioral therapy for tics to begin in two weeks. Next scheduled visit 2026-05-18: six-week depression review: sertraline dose increase; behavioral therapy for tics under way. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
